\id LUK - Aperto Bible (English) v5.2.0-inverted (2026-06-30)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Luke
\toc1 The Gospel of Luke
\toc2 Luke
\toc3 Lk
\mt1 Luke

\c 1

\s1 To Theophilus
\p
\v 1 Many before me have already taken up the task of setting down an orderly account\add , the way our historians frame a reliable record,\add* of the things that have come to their fullness among us,\add  brought to completion, not merely happened to,\add*\f + \fr 1:1 \fk TEXT \fq orderly account ... fullness \ft Luke opens with the conventions of a formal historiographical prologue, the kind Greek historians like Herodotus and Thucydides used to establish credibility. The verb behind "come to their fullness" (peplerophoremenon, perfect passive) signals events brought to completion by God, not mere historical occurrence. The "many" predecessors are honored, not dismissed: Luke joins a tradition rather than displacing it.\f*
\v 2 just as they were handed to us\add , passed down through a trusted chain,\add* by those who were there from the beginning, who saw it with their own eyes\add  and could vouch for it firsthand\add* and went on to serve the word they witnessed.\f + \fr 1:2 \fk KONTEXT \fq handed to us ... own eyes \ft "Handed to us" renders paredosan, the formal transmission of tradition (paradosis) prized in both Jewish and Greco-Roman teaching, where reliable accounts moved through known chains of teachers. "Saw it with their own eyes" renders autoptai, a technical word for eyewitnesses carrying legal and historiographical weight, the strongest credential an ancient writer could claim.\f*
\v 3 And so I too, having traced the whole course of it carefully from the start\add , investigating the sources myself,\add* resolved to write it out for you in order, most honored Theophilus,\add  whose patronage stands behind this work,\add*\f + \fr 1:3 \fk KULTUR \fq most honored Theophilus \ft "Most honored" (kratiste) is an honorific marking high social rank. A dedication like this was a social transaction: Theophilus, whose name means "lover of God," was likely the patron who funded the work's production and copying, and naming him honored both giver and recipient. The verb "traced carefully" points to active research, not passive reception of what Luke was handed.\f*
\v 4 so that you may know how firm the ground is beneath the things you were taught.\add  Not to settle every question by force, but to give you something solid to stand on.\add*\f + \fr 1:4 \fk TEXT \fq firm the ground ... taught \ft "How firm the ground is" renders asphaleia, grounded reliability you can stand on, not aggressive certainty that silences questions. "The things you were taught" renders katechethes, the root of "catechesis," formal religious instruction: Theophilus has already been taught and now seeks confirmation, so the aim is reasoned confidence, not blind faith.\f*

\s1 An Old Priest and an Impossible Promise
\p
\v 5 It happened back when Herod was king over Judea,\add  an Idumean propped up by Rome, paranoid and quick to kill,\add* and in those days there was a priest named Zechariah, from the division of Abijah,\add  the eighth of the twenty four rotations that took their turns serving in the Temple,\add* and his wife was a descendant of Aaron herself; her name was Elizabeth.\add  Two priestly bloodlines under one roof, the kind of house a village reads as marked by God's favor.\add*\f + \fr 1:5 \fk KONTEXT \fq Herod ... division of Abijah ... descendant of Aaron \ft "In the days of Herod" anchors the story in hard political reality: Herod the Great (reigned 37 to 4 BCE) was an Idumean client king installed by Rome, distrusted by many Jews and notorious for violent paranoia. The "division of Abijah" was the eighth of twenty four priestly courses (1 Chr 24:10); each served one week twice a year plus the major festivals, so priestly duty was intermittent. Both Zechariah (a priest) and Elizabeth (of Aaron's line) carry priestly descent, an unusually blessed pairing in the eyes of their community, which sharpens the shock of their childlessness.\f*
\v 6 Both of them lived right before God, walking faithfully in everything the Lord commanded, blameless.\f + \fr 1:6 \fk TEXT \fq right before God ... blameless \ft "Righteous" (dikaioi) here is covenant faithfulness, not moral perfection, and "blameless" (amemptoi) means without reproach in keeping the Torah. Luke piles up the language of exemplary piety precisely so the reader will not read their childlessness in the next verse as a hidden judgment from God.\f*
\v 7 But they had no child, because Elizabeth could not have children, and by now both of them were old.\add  In their world that emptiness was read as a curse, and the blame fell on the woman.\add*\f + \fr 1:7 \fk KULTUR \fq could not have children \ft The word translated "could not have children" (steira, barren) carries deep biblical resonance, recalling Sarah, Rebekah, Rachel, and Hannah, women whose barrenness God reversed to bring forth a child of promise. Socially, childlessness was devastating and was typically blamed on the wife, threatening the family line and her standing. The text vindicates Elizabeth and never frames her infertility as moral failure.\f*
\v 8 One day Zechariah's division was on duty, and he was serving as priest before God.
\v 9 As the priests did it, the lot fell to him to go into the sanctuary of the Lord and burn the incense.\add  The one task chosen by lot, the holiest a common priest could ever touch.\add* Out of a whole lifetime, this was his one turn.\f + \fr 1:9 \fk KONTEXT \fq the lot fell to him ... burn the incense \ft Incense was offered on the small golden altar inside the Holy Place, a space only priests could enter, morning and evening. With so many priests and so few openings, the duty was assigned by lot, and a given priest might be chosen once in a lifetime or never. For Zechariah this was the pinnacle of his priestly life, a privilege he could have waited decades for.\f*
\v 10 And the whole gathered crowd was praying outside, at the hour of incense.\add  He goes in alone; the people stay beyond the threshold while the smoke rises.\add*\f + \fr 1:10 \fk KULTUR \fq praying outside, at the hour of incense \ft The priest entered the Holy Place alone while the assembled worshipers prayed in the courts outside (cf. v.10). The rising incense was understood to carry the people's prayers upward, and the priest acted as their mediator before God, which is why this private moment is also a public, communal one.\f*
\v 11 Then an angel of the Lord appeared to him, standing at the right side of the altar of incense.\add  The right side, the side of favor and power.\add*\f + \fr 1:11 \fk KONTEXT \fq the right side of the altar \ft In the dim Holy Place, lit only by the seven branched lampstand, the angel stands at the right of the altar. The right side traditionally signals favor and strength, so the placement itself, before a word is spoken, marks this as a message of grace.\f*
\v 12 When Zechariah saw him he was shaken, and fear came over him.\add  Not mild surprise, the kind of dread that hits the body.\add*\f + \fr 1:12 \fk TEXT \fq he was shaken, and fear came over him \ft The Greek pairs etarachthe ("was troubled," deeply agitated) with phobos ("fear"), a visceral, physical terror, not gentle astonishment. This bodily fear is the standard human response in the Old Testament pattern of angelic announcements (cf. Gideon, the parents of Samson, Abraham).\f*
\v 13 But the angel said to him, "Don't be afraid, Zechariah. Your prayer has been heard.\add  God himself has heard it.\add* Your wife Elizabeth will bear you a son, and you are to name him John.\f + \fr 1:13 \fk TEXT \fq Your prayer has been heard \ft "Has been heard" is a divine passive (eisekousthe): the unspoken actor is God himself. The prayer in view can be read at once as Zechariah's longing for a child and as Israel's prayer for redemption, and Gabriel's answer in vv.13 to 17 covers both.\f*
\v 14 He will be your joy and delight, and many will rejoice at his birth,
\v 15 because he will be great in the sight of the Lord. He must never drink wine or strong drink,\add  set apart for God like a Nazirite from the womb,\add* and he will be filled with the Holy Spirit while he is still in his mother's womb.\f + \fr 1:15 \fk KONTEXT \fq He must never drink wine or strong drink \ft Lifelong abstinence from wine and strong drink echoes the Nazirite consecration (Num 6) and the dedication of figures like Samson and Samuel, marking John as set apart for God from the very start. That the Spirit fills him before birth signals the new era Luke is announcing, where the Spirit's work begins even in the womb.\f*
\v 16 He will turn many of the people of Israel back to the Lord their God.
\v 17 He will go out ahead of the Lord in the spirit and power of Elijah,\add  the prophet whose return would signal the day of the Lord,\add* to turn the hearts of parents back to their children and the disobedient back to the wisdom of those who live right, to make a people ready for the Lord."\f + \fr 1:17 \fk KONTEXT \fq the spirit and power of Elijah \ft This draws directly on Malachi 3:23 to 24 (4:5 to 6), where God promises to send Elijah before the great day of the Lord to turn parents' hearts to their children. To a first century Jewish ear, "the spirit and power of Elijah" announces nothing less than the long awaited dawn of God's decisive intervention. The body renders Malachi's gendered "fathers" inclusively as "parents," following the verse's own scope.\f*
\v 18 Zechariah said to the angel, "How can I be sure of this? I'm an old man, and my wife is well on in years."\add  The same question Abraham once asked, and from a priest who should know the old stories best.\add*\f + \fr 1:18 \fk HARM \fq How can I be sure of this? \ft Zechariah's request for proof echoes Abraham's "How am I to know?" (Gen 15:8). His doubt is presented honestly: even the righteous can struggle to believe. The silencing that follows is formational discipline within faith, not condemnation, and the narrative restores him by the end of the chapter.\f*
\v 19 The angel answered, "I am Gabriel. I stand in the presence of God, and I was sent to speak to you and bring you this good news.\add  One of the few who stand in God's own court, sent with the good news himself.\add*\f + \fr 1:19 \fk NAME \fq I am Gabriel \ft Gabriel is one of the archangels who "stand in God's presence" (cf. Dan 8 to 9); his name means roughly "God's strong one." The verb behind "bring good news" (euangelisasthai) is gospel vocabulary, surfacing this early in Luke. To name himself and his standing is to answer Zechariah's doubt with the full weight of heaven's authority.\f*
\v 20 And now, listen: you will be silent, unable to say a word, until the day this happens, because you did not believe my words, which will come true at their appointed time."\add  The priest who doubted the promise must now carry it in silence.\add*\f + \fr 1:20 \fk TEXT \fq you will be silent ... until the day this happens \ft The silence is both sign and discipline, and it is embodied, not merely symbolic: Zechariah will live wordless for some nine months until the child is born and named (resolved at v.64). The irony lands hard, the very priest whose role is to speak blessing over the people leaves the sanctuary unable to speak at all.\f*
\v 21 Meanwhile the people were waiting for Zechariah, wondering why he was taking so long in the sanctuary.\add  Far longer than the brief offering should take.\add*\f + \fr 1:21 \fk KULTUR \fq wondering why he was taking so long \ft The incense offering itself was brief, and priests did not linger in the Holy Place. The crowd's unease grows because the delay is abnormal, a quiet sign that something extraordinary has happened inside.\f*
\v 22 When he finally came out, he could not speak to them, and they realized he had seen a vision in the sanctuary. He kept making signs to them but stayed mute.\add  He could not give the blessing the people were waiting for.\add*\f + \fr 1:22 \fk KONTEXT \fq he could not speak to them \ft After offering incense the priest would normally come out to pronounce the blessing over the waiting people. Zechariah's inability to speak both confirms that he has encountered something divine and withholds the very blessing his office exists to deliver, so the crowd reads the silence correctly: he has seen a vision.\f*
\v 23 When his days of service were over, he went home.\add  Back to the hill country, where priests kept land and trade between rotations.\add*\f + \fr 1:23 \fk KULTUR \fq he went home \ft Because priests served only two weeks a year plus festivals, they lived ordinary working lives the rest of the time, often on land or in trade in the Judean hill country. Zechariah simply returns to that everyday life, now carrying the promise in silence.\f*
\v 24 Soon afterward his wife Elizabeth became pregnant, and for five months she kept herself out of sight. She said,
\v 25 "This is what the Lord has done for me. In these days he has looked on me and taken away the shame I carried in front of everyone."\add  The disgrace a barren woman bore before the whole village, lifted at last.\add*\f + \fr 1:25 \fk KULTUR \fq taken away the shame I carried in front of everyone \ft Elizabeth's "disgrace among people" reflects the real social stigma attached to childlessness, not any divine judgment. Her words are vindication: God has reversed her standing in the community's eyes. The seclusion may signal modesty, gratitude, or protection of the pregnancy, and the text leaves the reason open.\f*

\s1 Mary Says Yes
\p
\v 26 When Elizabeth was six months along, God sent the angel Gabriel to a town in Galilee called Nazareth, a place no one important had ever heard of,\add  a peasant village Rome had never bothered to learn the name of, which is exactly where the Most High chose to begin.\add*\f + \fr 1:26 \fk KONTEXT \fq a place no one important had ever heard of \ft Nazareth was a small agricultural village beneath the notice of Rome and largely of Jerusalem. Luke's reversal theme is already running: the supreme God selects a nobody from nowhere, not a royal house in a capital. \f*
\v 27 to a young woman pledged in marriage to a man named Joseph, a descendant of David. The pledge was binding, as good as marriage,\add  and Joseph's line is no incidental detail: it carries the throne of David straight into this child's legal name.\add* though she had never slept with a man. For a girl in her position, a pregnancy now would look like only one thing, betrayal, and a betrothed girl found pregnant could be denounced, cast off, even stoned. Her name was Mary.\f + \fr 1:27 \fk KONTEXT \fq a descendant of David \ft Betrothal was legally binding, broken only by divorce; the family's honor was at stake. Legal paternity through Joseph conveyed inheritance and lineage rights regardless of biological generation, which is how the child receives the Davidic claim (2 Sam 7). \f*
\v 28 The angel came to her and said, "Rejoice! God has poured out his favor on you, and it rests on you still.\add  You are already the graced one, and graced you remain.\add* The Lord is with you."\f + \fr 1:28 \fk TEXT \fq it rests on you still \ft "Graced one" renders the perfect passive participle kecharitomene: a completed action with an ongoing result. Mary has been favored and remains so. Origen noted this exact greeting is found nowhere else in Scripture, addressed to Mary alone. \f*
\v 29 The words shook her. She turned them over and over, trying to make sense of a greeting like that.\f + \fr 1:29 \fk TEXT \fq The words shook her \ft The verb dietarachthe denotes deep inner turmoil, not mild surprise, and dielogizeto pictures her reasoning the greeting through, an interior weighing rather than passive confusion. \f*
\v 30 "Don't be afraid, Mary," the angel said. "God has chosen to show you his favor.
\v 31 Listen: you are going to conceive and give birth to a son, and you are to name him Jesus.
\v 32 He will be great. He will be called the Son of the Most High, and the Lord God will give him the throne of his ancestor David.
\v 33 He will reign over the people of Jacob forever, and his kingdom will never end."\add  This is the promise sworn to David coming due: the throne re-established, now without end.\add*\f + \fr 1:33 \fk KONTEXT \fq his kingdom will never end \ft The piled titles, Son of the Most High, throne of David, an everlasting reign, announce the fulfillment of God's covenant promise to David (2 Sam 7). To a first hearer these were not generic honorifics but the long-awaited Messianic claim. \f*
\v 34 "How can this happen," Mary asked, "when I have never slept with a man?"\add  Not whether God could be trusted, but how this would actually come to be.\add* She knew what such a pregnancy would cost her, and still she stayed in the conversation.\f + \fr 1:34 \fk TEXT \fq How can this happen \ft Mary's question is inquiry, not doubt. She asks "how?" not "how can I be sure?" In contrast to Zechariah, her question is met with explanation rather than silencing. \f*
\v 35 The angel answered, "The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will settle over you, the way God's presence once filled the tent of meeting.\add  The same glory-cloud that overshadowed the tabernacle now overshadows you, and you become the place where God dwells.\add* So the child will be holy, and he will be called the Son of God.\f + \fr 1:35 \fk KONTEXT \fq the way God's presence once filled the tent of meeting \ft "Will settle over you" renders episkiasei, "overshadow," echoing the cloud that covered the tent of meeting so that the glory of the Lord filled it (Exod 40:35). Mary becomes the new locus of divine presence, and the Spirit, not human agency, is the agent of conception. \f*
\v 36 And here is something more: your relative Elizabeth, the one everyone called barren, is six months pregnant in her old age,\add  the proof is already breathing, a sign you can go and see for yourself.\add*\f + \fr 1:36 \fk KONTEXT \fq six months pregnant in her old age \ft Elizabeth's pregnancy is offered as a confirming sign. Faith here is not blind: the impossible has already begun in someone Mary knows, answering the unspoken question of how anyone could believe such news. \f*
\v 37 because no word that comes from God will ever fail."
\v 38 "I am the Lord's servant," Mary said.\add  His to command, by my own choosing and no one else's.\add* "I give myself to this. Let everything happen to me just as you have said." And the angel left her.\f + \fr 1:38 \fk TEXT \fq I am the Lord's servant \ft Doule is a female bondservant, but here it is Mary's own self-designation, a freely chosen radical availability to God, not subservience imposed by men. "Let everything happen to me" renders an optative of willing consent: a powerful active yes, her agency, not passive compliance. \f*

\s1 Two Women, Two Miracles
\p
\v 39 Soon after, Mary set out and hurried into the hill country of Judea, to a town there.\add  It was days of hard travel, the better part of a hundred miles from Nazareth, and she went like someone racing to see for herself whether the sign was true.\add*\f + \fr 1:39 \fk KULTUR \fq hurried into the hill country \ft The journey from Nazareth to the Judean hill country was roughly 80 to 100 miles, several days on foot through difficult terrain. Mary travels "with haste" (meta spoudes, urgency), which the commentators read as eagerness to confirm the sign Gabriel had given her: that the aged Elizabeth was herself pregnant.\f*
\v 40 She went straight to Zechariah's house and greeted Elizabeth.\add  Two women alone, far from the men and the priests, where no one was watching. This is where the new thing would first be spoken aloud.\add*\f + \fr 1:40 \fk KONTEXT \fq greeted Elizabeth \ft Women's gatherings were private, domestic space, outside official religious authority. Yet Luke sets the first prophetic utterances of the new era here, among two women, not in the temple. Zechariah, the priest, is still struck mute and cannot speak; it is Elizabeth who will prophesy.\f*
\v 41 The moment Elizabeth heard Mary's greeting, the baby leaped inside her,\add  the very leap the angel had promised, a child filled with the Spirit before he ever drew breath,\add* and Elizabeth was filled with the Holy Spirit.\f + \fr 1:41 \fk KONTEXT \fq the baby leaped inside her \ft The verb (eskirtesen) is strong, the leaping of lambs, not a gentle stir. This fulfills Gabriel's word that John would be filled with the Spirit even from his mother's womb (1:15). The unborn forerunner recognizes the unborn Lord: prophetic action before birth.\f*
\v 42 She cried out at the top of her voice:\add  no demure whisper but a prophet's shout,\add* "Of all women, you are the one God has blessed,\add  blessed as Jael was blessed, and Judith, the women through whom God delivered Israel,\add* and blessed is the child you carry!\f + \fr 1:42 \fk TEXT \fq Of all women, you are the one God has blessed \ft Elizabeth's cry (krauge megale, a loud shout) is prophetic intensity, not refined speech. Her blessing deliberately echoes the words spoken over Jael (Judges 5:24) and Judith (Judith 13:18), placing Mary among Israel's heroic women through whom God brought deliverance.\f*
\v 43 But who am I, that the mother of my Lord should come to me?\add  That she, an old priest's wife, should be the one honored by a visit from the young woman carrying her Master.\add*\f + \fr 1:43 \fk KULTUR \fq the mother of my Lord \ft By every social expectation the younger woman should defer to the elder, and a peasant girl to the wife of a priest. Elizabeth reverses it: she counts the visit an honor done to her. "My Lord" (kyrios) carries messianic and divine weight, an astonishing title acknowledging the unborn Jesus' superior status.\f*
\v 44 The instant your greeting reached my ears, the baby inside me leaped for joy.
\v 45 How happy is the woman who believed!\add  She trusted where another, my own husband, had doubted and been silenced.\add* Every word the Lord spoke to her will be brought to its full completion."\f + \fr 1:45 \fk TEXT \fq How happy is the woman who believed \ft A formal beatitude (makaria, blessed/fortunate). The blessing is for believing, not for biological privilege, and stands in pointed contrast to Zechariah, who doubted Gabriel and was struck mute (1:18-20). "Full completion" renders teleiosis: not merely that the promise will happen, but that it will be brought to perfect fulfillment.\f*

\s1 Mary's Song
\q1 \v 46 Everything in me celebrates God.
\q2 \v 47 My spirit dances over the One who saves me,
\q1 \v 48 because he looked at me,
\q2 a servant girl, a nobody,\add  the kind of person the world counts for nothing.\add*\f + \fr 1:48 \fk KULTUR \fq a servant girl, a nobody \ft The word behind this (tapeinosis) names a low social position, not a humble attitude. Mary is not boasting of her modesty; she is naming her place at the bottom: young, poor, female, and from an occupied backwater. That God should look there is the whole shock of the song.\f*
\q1 From now on, every generation will call me blessed,\add  this nobody, remembered for good.\add*
\q1 \v 49 for the Mighty One has done great things for me.
\q2 Holy is his name.
\q1 \v 50 His faithful love, that mercy he keeps,\add  the promise that holds even when everything else gives way,\add*\f + \fr 1:50 \fk TEXT \fq faithful love, that mercy he keeps \ft The Greek eleos here carries the weight of the Hebrew hesed: not soft feeling but covenant loyalty, a love bound by a sworn promise. It is the same steadfast love the psalms sing of, the love that keeps its word across generations.\f*
\q2 reaches from one generation to the next,
\q2 to all who stand in awe of him.
\q1 \v 51 He has acted. In raw strength. With his arm.\add  The way he split the sea and broke Pharaoh, he is doing again.\add*\f + \fr 1:51 \fk KONTEXT \fq In raw strength. With his arm \ft God's outstretched arm is the language of the Exodus, the same arm that shattered Egypt and brought slaves out free. Mary is not describing private comfort; she is announcing that the God who once overturned an empire is on the move once more.\f*
\q2 He has scattered the proud,
\q2 broken up the schemes they were so sure of.
\q1 \v 52 He has dragged the powerful down from their thrones\add  the rulers who lord it over us,\add*\f + \fr 1:52 \fk KULTUR \fq the powerful down from their thrones \ft The word here (dynastes) is the vocabulary of political power: kings, governors, the men who hold the thrones. Under Roman occupation, to sing that God topples the throne-holders and lifts the trodden-down is not a pious sentiment but dangerous talk, a hope for the whole order to be turned over.\f*
\q2 and lifted up the ones beneath everyone's feet.
\q1 \v 53 The hungry he has filled with good things,\add  real bread for the bellies that ache,\add*\f + \fr 1:53 \fk KULTUR \fq The hungry he has filled \ft In the villages of Galilee hunger was not a metaphor. A few families held the land and the wealth; the peasants lived one bad harvest from starvation. Mary names a concrete reversal of that gap, food for the empty and empty hands for the full, the kind of overturning the old Jubilee laws once pictured.\f*
\q2 and the rich he has sent away with empty hands.
\q1 \v 54 He has taken hold of Israel his servant,\add  his covenant child,\add*\f + \fr 1:54 \fk TEXT \fq Israel his servant \ft The word (pais) means both servant and child. It frames Israel not as a hired hand but as the people God bound himself to and will not let go, the family on whose behalf he is now stepping in.\f*
\q2 he has not forgotten his mercy, the love he pledged,
\q1 \v 55 just as he promised our ancestors,\add  the promise made to Abraham long ago and never withdrawn,\add*\f + \fr 1:55 \fk KONTEXT \fq just as he promised our ancestors \ft The closing lines reach all the way back to the oath sworn to Abraham. Everything God is doing now, the lowly lifted, the hungry fed, is the keeping of one ancient promise to one family, stretching unbroken into all the generations still to come.\f*
\q2 to Abraham and his children, forever.
\q1 \v 56 Mary stayed with Elizabeth about three months,
\q2 and then went back home.

\s1 His Name Is John
\p
\v 57 When Elizabeth's time came, she gave birth to a son.
\v 58 Her neighbors and relatives heard how great a mercy the Lord had shown her,\add  for in their eyes a barren old woman had carried the shame of God's withheld favor, and now that reproach was lifted,\add* and they celebrated with her.\f + \fr 1:58 \fk KULTUR \fq how great a mercy the Lord had shown her \ft In this culture childlessness was widely read as a sign of divine disfavor, a public reproach borne by the woman. Elizabeth's late pregnancy is therefore not merely happy news but the visible reversal of a presumed curse, "great mercy" (megalyno to eleos) the Lord had shown her. The neighbors gather to share in the honor restored to the household.\f*
\v 59 On the eighth day they came to circumcise the child,\add  the covenant sign that marked his formal entry into Israel, exactly as the Law required,\add* and they were ready to name him Zechariah, after his father.\f + \fr 1:59 \fk KONTEXT \fq On the eighth day they came to circumcise the child \ft Circumcision on the eighth day was the non-negotiable covenant requirement (Leviticus 12:3, Genesis 17:12), John's formal entry into the people of Israel. The ceremony took place in the home as a public, communal celebration, and it customarily included the naming. Naming a son after his father or grandfather preserved family identity, so the community fully expected a "Zechariah the younger."\f*
\v 60 But his mother cut in. "No," she said. "He is to be called John."\add  The community had a name ready and a custom on its side; she had a word from God, and she would not be talked out of it.\add*\f + \fr 1:60 \fk KULTUR \fq his mother cut in \ft Elizabeth's intervention is doubly surprising: she breaks the family-naming convention, and she does so as a woman, when control of naming was not hers to exercise. That she already insists on "John" signals that she knows the angelic message given to Zechariah, whether through his written communication or prophetic insight. The name itself, Yohanan, means "the LORD has shown grace," a one-word summary of the whole story.\f*
\v 61 "But no one in your family has that name," they objected.
\v 62 So they turned to his father, motioning to ask what he wanted to call him.\add  They appealed to the man of the house, certain he would set the matter right and overrule his wife.\add*\f + \fr 1:62 \fk KULTUR \fq So they turned to his father \ft The community appeals to Zechariah as the family's male head, expecting him to override Elizabeth and restore the customary name. They gesture rather than speak to him, which suggests he was understood to be deaf as well as mute. The expectation makes what follows a second surprise: he sides with his wife.\f*
\v 63 He asked for a writing tablet, and to everyone's astonishment he wrote, "His name is John."\add  Not "let him be called," but a settled fact: the name is already his, given from heaven before it was ever spoken on earth.\add*\f + \fr 1:63 \fk TEXT \fq His name is John \ft The Greek uses the present tense ("John is his name"), a declaration of established fact rather than a proposal up for debate. The writing tablet (pinakidion) was a small wood-and-wax board. By choosing the heaven-given name over the family name, Zechariah confirms in writing the obedience his earlier doubt had withheld.\f*
\v 64 At that very moment his mouth was opened and his tongue set free, and he began to speak, praising God.\add  His first words after the long silence were not complaint but blessing, the speech of a man whose faith had been remade in the waiting.\add*\f + \fr 1:64 \fk TEXT \fq his mouth was opened ... praising God \ft "His mouth was opened" is a divine passive: God restores his speech the instant he obeys. "Immediately" (parachrema) is Luke's signature for sudden divine action. That his very first utterance is blessing (eulogon), not the complaint of a man freed from punishment, marks how the silence had formed rather than merely disciplined him.\f*
\v 65 Fear settled over all the neighbors,\add  the awe that falls on people when they sense God himself has drawn near,\add* and through the whole hill country of Judea people kept talking about everything that had happened.\f + \fr 1:65 \fk KONTEXT \fq Fear settled over all the neighbors \ft The "fear" (phobos) is reverent awe at a manifest act of God, not terror. Such wonder spreads: the report travels through the Judean hill country, preparing the wider community for the role this child will one day play.\f*
\v 66 And all who heard it turned it over in their hearts and asked, "What is this child going to be?" For the hand of the Lord was with him.\add  The hand of the LORD: the very power and favor that had carried Israel out of Egypt now rested on this child.\add*\f + \fr 1:66 \fk TEXT \fq the hand of the Lord was with him \ft "The hand of the LORD" (cheir kyriou) is an Old Testament idiom for God's active power and favor (for example Exodus 13:3, Ezra 7:9). The phrase signals divine commissioning: this child is set apart for God's purposes, and the question "What then will this child become?" hangs over the rest of the story.\f*

\s1 Zechariah's Prophecy
\q1 \v 67 And his father Zechariah, flooded with the Holy Spirit, began to prophesy\add  the silenced priest, his tongue loosed at last, now speaking for God:\add*\f + \fr 1:67 \fk KONTEXT \fq began to prophesy \ft Zechariah has been mute since the angel's word in the temple. His first full speech is not small talk but Spirit-filled prophecy, the elderly priest who could not speak now opening with words that belong to the great prophets before him.\f*
\q1 \v 68 Praise the Lord, the God of Israel,\add  blessed be he, in the words our prayers have always begun,\add*\f + \fr 1:68 \fk KULTUR \fq Praise the Lord, the God of Israel \ft This is the opening of a berakah, the blessing-form every Jewish prayer used (compare 1 Kings 1:48; Psalm 41:13). A worshipper in the synagogue would recognize the cadence at once: Zechariah, the priest, is praying in the very shape of his liturgical world.\f*
\q2 for he has come to his people and bought them back.\add  He has visited us at last, paying the ransom to set his people free, as he once bought us out of Egypt.\add*\f + \fr 1:68 \fk TEXT \fq he has come to his people and bought them back \ft Two loaded words sit here. "Visited" (episkeptomai) is what God does when he finally steps into his people's plight to act. "Bought back" (lytrosis) is ransom language, the price paid to free a captive, the same word that carries the memory of the Exodus, when God redeemed slaves and made them his own.\f*
\q1 \v 69 He has raised up a mighty Savior for us\add  a horn of rescue, strength lifted high,\add*\f + \fr 1:69 \fk TEXT \fq a mighty Savior, a horn of rescue \ft The image is literally a "horn of salvation." A horn is the raised strength of a charging animal, and also the horn of oil used to anoint a king. To raise a horn is to raise up power that saves, and to raise it "in the house of David" is to name the long-awaited king from David's line.\f*
\q2 in the house of his servant David,\add  the royal line we have waited generations to see restored.\add*\f + \fr 1:69 \fk KONTEXT \fq in the house of his servant David \ft Israel's hope had long centered on a descendant of David who would restore the kingdom. Under Roman occupation that hope was electric. Naming David's house is naming the promised king, even as the song will soon redirect what kind of king he is.\f*
\q1 \v 70 just as he promised long ago
\q2 through the mouths of his holy prophets:
\q1 \v 71 rescue from our enemies,
\q2 out of the grip of everyone who hates us.\add  Deliverance from the powers that hold us down,\add*\f + \fr 1:71 \fk KONTEXT \fq our enemies, everyone who hates us \ft For a people living under Roman occupation, "enemies" had an obvious face. The hope was real liberation from foreign domination. Yet the song will not end in conquest: its final word is peace, and Luke quietly reshapes the dream of victory into something deeper than driving out an army.\f*
\q1 \v 72 He is keeping faith with our ancestors,
\q2 remembering his holy covenant,
\q1 \v 73 the oath he swore to Abraham our father:\add  the promise that makes us one people, bound to him by ancestry, not saved one by one but together,\add*\f + \fr 1:73 \fk KULTUR \fq the oath he swore to Abraham our father \ft Belonging to God ran through descent from Abraham; the covenant was a family bond, not a private contract. Zechariah is singing of corporate rescue, the whole people redeemed at once because of a promise sworn to their common father.\f*
\q1 \v 74 to pull us free from the hands of our enemies
\q2 so we can serve him unafraid,\add  free to stand before him in worship, the fear gone,\add*\f + \fr 1:74 \fk TEXT \fq so we can serve him unafraid \ft The word for "serve" (latreuo) is the language of priestly worship at the temple. The goal of rescue is not merely safety but unhindered, unafraid worship, exactly the world Zechariah the priest has spent his life inside.\f*
\q1 \v 75 holy and right before him
\q2 every day we live.
\q1 \v 76 And you, little one, you will be called a prophet of the Most High,\add  you, a priest's son, marked out not for the altar but for the road ahead of one greater,\add*\f + \fr 1:76 \fk KONTEXT \fq And you, little one, a prophet of the Most High \ft The song turns from Israel's king to Zechariah's own infant. As a priest's son, John would have been expected to follow his father into temple service. Instead he is named a prophet who runs ahead of the Lord, the Levite's child pointing beyond himself to the greater one from David's line.\f*
\q2 for you will walk out ahead of the Lord to clear his road,
\q1 \v 77 to let his people know they are saved
\q2 because their sins are forgiven.
\q1 \v 78 This is the gut-deep mercy of our God,\add  mercy that turns him over inside, the way a parent aches for a child,\add*\f + \fr 1:78 \fk TEXT \fq the gut-deep mercy of our God \ft The phrase is literally "bowels of mercy." The ancients located deep feeling in the gut, so this names compassion that is visceral, not polite, the kind that moves a person from the inside. From mercy this deep comes everything that follows.\f*
\q2 the mercy by which the Dawn from on high will break over us,\add  the Rising One, dayspring and promised Branch from David's stock, coming up like the sun,\add*\f + \fr 1:78 \fk TEXT \fq the Dawn from on high \ft One Greek word (anatole) holds two pictures at once: the rising of the sun, and the messianic "Branch" sprouting from David's line (Jeremiah 23:5; Zechariah 3:8; 6:12; the "sun of righteousness" of Malachi 4:2). Zechariah's hearers caught both, the dawn that ends the night and the king who springs from the old root.\f*
\q1 \v 79 rising on all who sit in darkness, in the shadow of death,\add  light reaching those left in the dark, the oppressed, the poor, the forgotten,\add*\f + \fr 1:79 \fk KONTEXT \fq all who sit in darkness, in the shadow of death \ft This is the language of Isaiah 9:2, light dawning on a people in darkness, and of Psalm 23:4, the valley of death's shadow. "Those who sit in darkness" are not only the spiritually lost but the crushed and overlooked of an occupied land. The dawn comes first to them.\f*
\q2 to set our feet on the road that leads to peace.\add  not the peace of a won war but shalom, the whole world set right.\add*\f + \fr 1:79 \fk TEXT \fq the road that leads to peace \ft The final word is peace (eirene), carrying the Hebrew shalom: not merely the absence of fighting but wholeness, everything made right. After all the talk of enemies, the path leads not to violence but here, feet guided into shalom.\f*

\s1 Growing Up in the Wilderness
\p
\v 80 The child kept growing, and grew strong in spirit,\add the Spirit shaping him from within, not the body alone but the prophet taking form.\add*\f + \fr 1:80 \fk TEXT \fq grew strong in spirit \ft The Greek ekrataiouto is passive: the child was being strengthened. Paired with "in spirit," the phrase points past physical growth to spiritual maturation, the hidden formational work that prepares him for what is coming.\f* He lived out in the wilderness country, far from the towns,\add the ground where prophets are made: Moses, Elijah, Israel itself were each forged in the wastes, away from Temple and city, and came back carrying a word no one in power had given them.\add*\f + \fr 1:80 \fk KONTEXT \fq the wilderness country \ft The plural eremois means uninhabited regions, not a single lifeless desert; he moved among the sparsely settled Judean wilderness. Scripture reads the wilderness as the place of prophetic formation (Moses, Elijah, Israel). John's shaping happens outside institutional religion, away from the Temple though he is a priest's son, and from there he will emerge to confront Jerusalem's leadership. Some have speculated contact with the Essene community at Qumran, but the text gives no warrant for it.\f* until the day he stepped into the open before Israel,\add the day he was presented, formally shown to the nation as the one they had been waiting for.\add*\f + \fr 1:80 \fk TEXT \fq stepped into the open before Israel \ft The noun anadeixis denotes a public showing or formal commissioning, the official beginning of his ministry. The verse holds the question raised earlier, "What then will this child become?" (1:66), unanswered, leaving the reader waiting until he appears in chapter 3.\f*
